<title>SOUTH KOREA: South Korea's 10-month energy demand up 9.9 pct.</title>
<headline>South Korea's 10-month energy demand up 9.9 pct.</headline>
<dateline>SEOUL 1996-12-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>South Korea's energy demand grew 9.9 percent to 131.88 million tonnes of oil equivalent (TOE) in the first 10 months of 1996 from 119.95 million a year ago, the Ministry of Trade, Industry and Energy said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>Increasing use for liquefied natural gas (LNG) for home fuel and nuclear power was behind the growth, a ministry statement said.</p>
<p>Oil products consumption growth slowed, however, due to industrial slowdown, it said.  </p>
<p>Breakdown of South Korea's demand for energy resources in the first 10 months, compared with the same period in 1995. (Figures in brackets are year-on-year growth in percentage):</p>
<p>				Jan-Oct 1996	 Jan-Oct 1995</p>
<p>				Amount		  Amount</p>
<p> Total		     131,879   (9.9)     119,954   (8.9)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 TOE)</p>
<p> Petroleum products    578,017   (7.5)     537,499   (8.8)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 barrels)</p>
<p> LNG			   7,259  (35.0)	 5,378  (19.7)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 tonnes)</p>
<p> Hard coal		   3,582 (-17.0)	 4,316 (-20.7)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 tonnes)</p>
<p> Bituminous coal	  35,078  (12.1)	31,287   (8.5)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 tonnes)</p>
<p> Nuclear power	    61,610  (12.7)	54,682  (10.9)</p>
<p>(in gigawatt hours)</p>
<p> Hydroelectric power     4,486  (-5.0)	 4,722  (33.4)</p>
<p>(in gigawatt hours)</p>
<p> Other			   941  (10.9)	   848  (16.2)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 TOE)</p>
<p>(Source: Ministry of Trade, Industry and Energy).</p>
</text>
